eUnitedMarketplaceServicesReport\",\"id\":\"235\"},{\"name\":\"Closing documents\",\"href\":\"en/reference/reports/generateClosureDocumentsReport\",\"id\":\"236\"},{\"name\":\"Report on convergence with closing documents\",\"href\":\"en/reference/reports/generateClosureDocumentsDetalizationReport\",\"id\":\"237\"},{\"name\":\"Marketing Account Report\",\"href\":\"en/ref","error":null,"capabilityHits":["routing","refunds","user-auth","oauth","parcel-shipping","tracking","ride-hailing","analytics-tracking","object-storage"]},"yandex-appmetrica":{"url":"https://appmetrica.yandex.com/docs/en/mobile-api/","fetchedAt":"2026-08-30T05:31:43.315Z","text":"Are you not a robot? Please confirm that you are not a robot We&#x27;re sorry, but it looks like requests sent from your device are automated. Why might this happen? JavaScript is disabled. After checkbox you will have additional check. How to enable JavaScript? I&#x27;m not a robot Press to continue Yandex SmartCaptcha If you have any problems, please use the feedback form 9314741247031798572 : 1788067903","error":null,"capabilityHits":[]},"yandex-metrica-reports":{"url":"https://yandex.com/dev/metrika/en/stat/","fetchedAt":"2026-08-30T05:31:43.448Z","text":"Introduction | Yandex Metrica API Interface reference FAQ Introduction API architecture Authorization Quick start Additional scenarios API error descriptions Changelog Management API Data import API Reporting API Introduction Dimensions Metrics Presets Segmentation Parametrization Localization and interpretation of dimension values Object IDs Sampling Examples List of dimensions and metrics Reporting API reference Logs API FAQ Feedback Introduction In this article : Compatibility of dimensions and metrics Types of reports Format of reports Data disclosure How do I determine if an API response contains data with limited disclosure? Reporting API Introduction Introduction Compatibility of dimensions and metrics Types of reports Format of reports Data disclosure How do I determine if an API response contains data with limited disclosure? The Reports API allows you to get site traffic statistics and other data without using the Yandex Metrica interface. API requests are created using dimensions and metrics. A dimension is an attribute of a session or hit that can be used for grouping data. In API requests, dimensions are set in the dimensions parameter. If you need to list multiple dimensions, separate them with commas. It is also possible to make a report without dimensions; in this case, the total result is calculated. A metric is a numerical value that is based on an attribute of a hit or session. In API requests, metrics are set in the metrics parameter. If you need to list multiple metrics, separate them with commas. More about terminology Let's explore the concepts of &quot;dimension&quot; and &quot;metric&quot; in more detail using Yandex Metrica's &quot;Operating systems&quot; report as an example: Operating system Page depth Windows 4.2 iOS 3.1 Linux 1.6 Where Operating system is an attribute of a session that is used for grouping report data (a dimension ). Page depth is a value that's calculated from numeric session attributes ( metric ) and corresponds to the specified dimension. Note If you are familiar with SQL, you can think of dimensions as columns used for grouping data, and metrics as the results returned by aggregate functions. For example, the report shown above can be imagined as a query to a hypothetical table of sessions: SELECT operatingSystem, avg(depth) from visits GROUP BY operatingSystem You can create the desired report structure by specifying metrics and dimensions in the API request. For example, to get a report on page depth with data grouped by OS type and version, use this request: https://api-metrika.yandex.net/stat/v1/data.csv?id=44147844&amp;metrics=ym:s:avgPageViews&amp;dimensions=ym:s:operatingSystem&amp;limit=5 Compatibility of dimensions and metrics The API supports two types of groupings and metrics: Sessions, with the prefix ym:s: . Hits have the ym:pv: prefix. You can't use different prefixes in the same request or specify more than one set (see Segmentation. Sets and relationships ). Alert You can specify a prefix different from the one in the request and use various sets when applying the filters parameter to filter retrieved data by dimension. For example, this request generates a report on the number of sessions and unique users from search engines for the page specified in the request: https://api-metrika.yandex.net/stat/v1/data?dimensions=ym:s:searchEngineName&amp;metrics=ym:s:visits,ym:s:users&amp;filters=ym:s:trafficSourceName=='Search engine traffic' AND ym:pv:URL=@'help'&amp;id=44147844 where dimensions=ym:s:searchEngineName is data grouped by the ym:s:searchEngine parameter (type of search engine). metrics=ym:s:visits,ym:s:users — Number of sessions and users. filters=ym:s:trafficSourceName=='Traffic from search engines' AND ym:pv:URL=@'help' — Data segmentation by the dimensions ym:s:trafficSourceName and ym:pv:URL . Types of reports Resulting data can be provided in the following formats: Table Drill down By time period Table comparison Drilldown comparison All report levels and metrics are shown as a table. To display data as a table, use the /stat/v1/data method. Generating a single branch of a tree view report. To create branches, use the /stat/v1/data/drilldown method. Data is divided over units of time (such as days, weeks, and months). This format is convenient for building charts and tracking trends. Use the /stat/v1/data/bytime method. Comparison of segments specified in the same request. Data is shown as a table. Use the /stat/v1/data/comparison method. Comparison of segments specified in the same request. Data is shown in a tree-view report. Use the /stat/v1/data/comparison/drilldown method. Format of reports The API returns responses in UTF-8 encoding. Responses are in JSON or CSV format. The format is specified in the request after the URL: GET https://api-metrika.yandex.net/stat/v1/data.**csv**?&lt;counter_id&gt;&amp;&lt;metrics&gt;&amp;&lt;dimensions&gt; Since JSON is the default format, it can be omitted: GET https://api-metrika.yandex.net/stat/v1/data?&lt;counter_id&gt;&amp;&lt;metrics&gt;&amp;&lt;dimensions&gt; Data disclosure Yandex Metrica protects users' privacy and ensures that all collected information is depersonalized. For this reason, certain information such as social-demographic data (gender, age, and so on) has limited disclosure. Such data is provided only if there were more than 10 users in the sample. Learn more about statistics depersonalization . For example, you want to know the percentage of males out of the total users for the day. At the time of the request, the site had 5 visitors (fewer than 10). In this case, the response will contain information about the total number of users for the day, but data will not be available for the number of males. How do I determine if an API response contains data with limited disclosure? The API response includes a contains_sensitive_data parameter. If its value is true , the provided data has limited disclosure. Was the article helpful? Yes No Previous Deleting a token Next Traffic module = {}; __NONCE__ = \"TOjg7PyNVqfIBQAK34+Lbg==\"; __ENV__ = \"production\"; __DATA__ = {\"err\":null,\"page\":\"/_doc\",\"pathname\":\"dev/metrika/en/stat/\",\"query\":{},\"props\":{\"pageProps\":{\"data\":{\"revision\":\"r20856727\",\"docLang\":\"en\",\"docLangs\":[\"ru\",\"en\"],\"docVersion\":null,\"docVersions\":null,\"viewerConfigParams\":{\"buildParams\":{\"strict\":true,\"breaks\":false,\"allowHTML\":true,\"singlePage\":true,\"apply-presets\":true,\"langs\":[\"ru\",\"en\"]},\"project-name\":\"dev-metrika\",\"is-new-external\":true,\"no-index\":false,\"langs\":[\"ru\",\"en\"],\"metrika\":19792447,\"favicon-src\":\"https://yastatic.net/s3/metrika/ui-projects/public/_/favicon.ico\"},\"viewerInterface\":{},\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eThe Reports API allows you to get site traffic statistics and other data without using the Yandex Metrica interface.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eAPI requests are created using dimensions and metrics.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eA \\u003cem\\u003edimension\\u003c/em\\u003e is an attribute of a session or hit that can be used for grouping data.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eIn API requests, dimensions are set in the \\u003ccode class=\\\"yfm-clipboard-inline-code\\\" role=\\\"button\\\" tabindex='0' id=\\\"inline-code-id-tjbm51ro\\\"\\u003edimensions\\u003c/code\\u003e parameter. If you need to list multiple dimensions, separate them with commas.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eIt is also possible to make a report without dimensions; in this case, the total result is calculated.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eA \\u003cem\\u003emetric\\u003c/em\\u003e is a numerical value that is based on an attribute of a hit or session.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eIn API requests, metrics are set in the \\u003ccode class=\\\"yfm-clipboard-inline-code\\\" role=\\\"button\\\" tabindex='0' id=\\\"inline-code-id-jxe0mrvr\\\"\\u003emetrics\\u003c/code\\u003e parameter. If you need to list multiple metrics, separate them with commas.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cdetails class=\\\"yfm-cut\\\"\\u003e\\u003csummary class=\\\"yfm-cut-title\\\"\\u003eMore about terminology\\u003c/summary\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"yfm-cut-content\\\"\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eLet's explore the concepts of \\u0026quot;dimension\\u0026quot; and \\u0026quot;metric\\u0026quot; in more detail using Yandex Metrica's \\u0026quot;Operating systems\\u0026quot; report as an example:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ctable\\u003e\\n\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eOperating system\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003ePage depth\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eWindows\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003e4.2\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eiOS\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003e3.1\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eLinux\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003e1.6\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\n\\u003c/table\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eWhere\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cul\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ccode class=\\\"yfm-clipboard-inline-code\\\" role=\\\"button\\\" tabindex='0' id=\\\"inline-code-id-62t4j3g0\\\"\\u003eOperating system\\u003c/code\\u003e is an attribute of a session that is used for grouping report data (a \\u003cem\\u003edimension\\u003c/em\\u003e).\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ccode class=\\\"yfm-clipboard-inline-code\\\" role=\\\"button\\\" tabindex='0' id=\\\"inline-code-id-3wm5w15m\\\"\\u003ePage depth\\u003c/code\\u003e is a value that's calculated from numeric session attributes (\\u003cem\\u003emetric\\u003c/em\\u003e) and corresponds to the specified dimension.\\u003c/li\\u003e\\n\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\n\\u003cdiv class=\\\"yfm-note yfm-accent-info\\\" note-type=\\\"info\\\"\\u003e\\u003cp class=\\\"yfm-note-title\\\"\\u003eNote\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cdiv class=\\\"yfm-note-content\\\"\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf you are familiar with SQL, you can think of dimensions as columns used for grouping data, and metrics as the results returned by aggregate functions.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eFor example, the report shown above can be imagined as a query to a hypothetical table of sessions:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n \\u003cdiv class=\\\"yfm-code-floating-container\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cpre\\u003e\\u003ccode class=\\\"hljs xml\\\"\\u003eSELECT operatingSystem, avg(depth) from visits GROUP BY operatingSystem\\n\\u003c/code\\u003e\\u003c/pre\\u003e\\n\\n \\u003cdiv class=\\\"yfm-code-floating\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cbutton role=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"g-button g-button_view_flat g-button_size_m g-button_pin_round-round g-md-viewer-code-button yfm-code-button yfm-wrapping-button\\\" tabindex=\\\"-1\\\" type=\\\"button\\\" aria-label=\\\"Toggle line wrapping\\\" aria-pressed=\\\"false\\\" data-tabindex=\\\"0\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cspan class=\\\"g-button__icon\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cspan class=\\\"g-button__icon-inner\\\"\\u003e\\u003csvg xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\\" xmlns:xlink=\\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\\" width=\\\"16\\\" height=\\\"16\\\" class=\\\"g-icon\\\" fill=\\\"currentColor\\\" stroke=\\\"none\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003csvg class=\\\"yfm-code-icon yfm-wrapping-icon\\\" xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\\" fill=\\\"none\\\" viewBox=\\\"0 0 16 16\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cpath fill=\\\"currentColor\\\" fill-rule=\\\"evenodd\\\" d=\\\"M2.47 11.28a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.06l3-3a.75.75 0 0 1 1.06 1.06L4.81 10H9a3.25 3.25 0 0 0 0-6.5H8A.75.75 0 0 1 8 2h1a4.75 4.75 0 1 1 0 9.5H4.81l1.72 1.72a.75.75 0 1 1-1.06 1.06z\\\" clip-rule=\\\"evenodd\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003c/path\\u003e\\n \\u003c/svg\\u003e\\n \\u003c/span\\u003e\\n \\u003c/span\\u003e\\n \\u003c/button\\u003e\\n \\u003cbutton role=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"g-button g-button_view_flat g-button_size_m g-button_pin_round-round g-md-viewer-code-button yfm-code-button yfm-clipboard-button\\\" aria-label=\\\"Copy\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cspan class=\\\"g-button__icon g-clipboard-button__icon\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cspan class=\\\"g-button__icon-inner\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003csvg width=\\\"16\\\" height=\\\"16\\\" viewBox=\\\"0 0 24 24\\\" class=\\\"yfm-code-icon yfm-clipboard-icon\\\" data-animation=\\\"107\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cpath\\n fill=\\\"currentColor\\\"\\n d=\\\"M19,21H8V7H19M19,5H8A2,2 0 0,0 6,7V21A2,2 0 0,0 8,23H19A2,2 0 0,0 21,21V7A2,2 0 0,0 19,5M16,1H4A2,2 0 0,0 2,3V17H4V3H16V1Z\\\"\\n /\\u003e\\n \\u003cpath\\n stroke=\\\"currentColor\\\"\\n fill=\\\"transparent\\\"\\n stroke-width=\\\"1.5\\\"\\n d=\\\"M9.5 13l3 3l5 -5\\\"\\n visibility=\\\"hidden\\\"\\n \\u003e\\n \\u003canimate\\n id=\\\"visibileAnimation-107\\\"\\n attributeName=\\\"visibility\\\"\\n from=\\\"hidden\\\"\\n to=\\\"visible\\\"\\n dur=\\\"0.2s\\\"\\n fill=\\\"freeze\\\"\\n begin=\\\"\\\"\\n /\\u003e\\n \\u003canimate\\n id=\\\"hideAnimation-107\\\"\\n attributeName=\\\"visibility\\\"\\n from=\\\"visible\\\"\\n to=\\\"hidden\\\"\\n dur=\\\"1s\\\"\\n begin=\\\"visibileAnimation-107.end+1\\\"\\n fill=\\\"freeze\\\"\\n /\\u003e\\n \\u003c/path\\u003e\\n \\u003c/svg\\u003e\\n \\u003c/span\\u003e\\n \\u003c/span\\u003e\\n \\u003c/button\\u003e\\n \\u003c/div\\u003e\\n \\u003c/div\\u003e\\n\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003c/details\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eYou can create the desired report structure by specifying metrics and dimensions in the API request.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eFor example, to get a report on page depth with data grouped by OS type and version, use this request:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\n \\u003cdiv class=\\\"yfm-code-floating-container\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cpre\\u003e\\u003ccode class=\\\"hljs\\\"\\u003ehttps://api-metrika.yandex.net/stat/v1/data.csv?id=44147844\\u0026amp;metrics=ym:s:avgPageViews\\u0026amp;dimensions=ym:s:operatingSystem\\u0026amp;limit=5\\n\\u003c/code\\u003e\\u003c/pre\\u003e\\n\\n \\u003cdiv class=\\\"yfm-code-floating\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cbutton role=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"g-button g-button_view_flat g-button_size_m g-button_pin_round-round g-md-viewer-code-button yfm-code-button yfm-wrapping-button\\\" tabindex=\\\"-1\\\" type=\\\"button\\\" aria-label=\\\"Toggle line wrapping\\\" aria-pressed=\\\"false\\\" data-tabindex=\\\"0\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cspan class=\\\"g-button__icon\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cspan class=\\\"g-button__icon-inner\\\"\\u003e\\u003csvg xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\\" xmlns:xlink=\\\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\\\" width=\\\"16\\\" height=\\\"16\\\" class=\\\"g-icon\\\" fill=\\\"currentColor\\\" stroke=\\\"none\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003csvg class=\\\"yfm-code-icon yfm-wrapping-icon\\\" xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\\" fill=\\\"none\\\" viewBox=\\\"0 0 16 16\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cpath fill=\\\"currentColor\\\" fill-rule=\\\"evenodd\\\" d=\\\"M2.47 11.28a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.06l3-3a.75.75 0 0 1 1.06 1.06L4.81 10H9a3.25 3.25 0 0 0 0-6.5H8A.75.75 0 0 1 8 2h1a4.75 4.75 0 1 1 0 9.5H4.81l1.72 1.72a.75.75 0 1 1-1.06 1.06z\\\" clip-rule=\\\"evenodd\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003c/path\\u003e\\n \\u003c/svg\\u003e\\n \\u003c/span\\u003e\\n \\u003c/span\\u003e\\n \\u003c/button\\u003e\\n \\u003cbutton role=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"g-button g-button_view_flat g-button_size_m g-button_pin_round-round g-md-viewer-code-button yfm-code-button yfm-clipboard-button\\\" aria-label=\\\"Copy\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cspan class=\\\"g-button__icon g-clipboard-button__icon\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cspan class=\\\"g-button__icon-inner\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003csvg width=\\\"16\\\" height=\\\"16\\\" viewBox=\\\"0 0 24 24\\\" class=\\\"yfm-code-icon yfm-clipboard-icon\\\" data-animation=\\\"118\\\"\\u003e\\n \\u003cpath\\n fill=\\\"currentColor\\\"\\n d=\\\"M19,21H8V7H19M19,5H8A2,2 0 0,0 6,7V21A2,2 0 0,0 8,23H19A2,2 0 0,0 21,21V7A2,2 0 0,0 19,5M16,1H4A2,2 0 0,0 2,3V17H4V3H16V1Z\\\"\\n /\\u003e\\n \\","error":null,"capabilityHits":["tracking","statistics","population-stats","analytics-tracking","object-storage"]},"yandex-partner-network":{"url":"https://yandex.com/dev/partner/doc/dg/","fetchedAt":"2026-09-02T07:12:09.892Z","text":"","error":"HTTP 404","capabilityHits":[]},"yandex-direct-reports":{"url":"https://yandex.com/dev/direct/doc/en/reports/reports","fetchedAt":"2026-09-02T07:12:09.697Z","text":"","error":"HTTP 404","capabilityHits":[]},"yandex-metrica-data-import":{"url":"https://yandex.com/dev/metrika/en/data-import/","fetchedAt":"2026-08-30T05:31:44.149Z","text":"Introduction | Yandex Metrica API Interface reference FAQ Introduction API architecture Authorization Quick start Additional scenarios API error descriptions Changelog Management API Data import API Introduction Importing expenses Importing customer and order data from the CRM Importing calls Importing offline conversions Importing chats Importing user parameters Measurement Protocol Reporting API Logs API FAQ Feedback Introduction In this article : Importing offline conversions Importing chats Importing data from a CRM Importing calls Importing expenses Importing user parameters Data import API Introduction Introduction Importing offline conversions Importing chats Importing data from a CRM Importing calls Importing expenses Importing user parameters Importing data into Yandex Metrica offers you more comprehensive insights into users or traffic sources from your tag. This can help you associate user actions on the site with their activity off the site, build end-to-end analytics, or assess your expenses across different traffic channels, significantly expanding your data analysis capabilities. Importing offline conversions Importing offline conversions allows you to transmit data about user actions outside the site, such as paying for services after filling out a site form. This data can be attributed to site sessions, helping you build end-to-end analytics and optimize your advertising efforts. Learn more about uploading offline conversions Importing chats Importing chats helps you track information about messenger chats with customers and attribute them to site sessions. You can evaluate both the number and quality of chats, track goals, and build reports based on the gathered data. Learn more about uploading chats Importing data from a CRM Importing data from your CRM allows you to upload information about customers and their orders to Yandex Metrica. This allows for a more detailed analysis based on order data and transaction statuses, and enables you to create segments for targeting. Learn more about uploading data from a CRM Importing calls Importing calls helps you track information about customer phone calls and attribute them to site sessions. You can evaluate both the number and quality of calls, track goals, and build reports based on the gathered data. Learn more about uploading calls Importing expenses Importing data about expenses allows you to compare advertising costs with campaign results, helping you optimize your budget and measure your return on investment (ROI). Learn more about uploading expenses Importing user parameters You can import demographic, behavioral, and other user data for more accurate audience segmentation and user behavior analysis. Learn more about importing user parameters Was the article helpful? Yes No Previous Disabling export for the tag Next Uploading expenses as multipart/form-data module = {}; __NONCE__ = \"7RXDN3yiIwLltaOu2TG5bw==\"; __ENV__ = \"production\"; __DATA__ = {\"err\":null,\"page\":\"/_doc\",\"pathname\":\"dev/metrika/en/data-import/\",\"query\":{},\"props\":{\"pageProps\":{\"data\":{\"revision\":\"r20856727\",\"docLang\":\"en\",\"docLangs\":[\"ru\",\"en\"],\"docVersion\":null,\"docVersions\":null,\"viewerConfigParams\":{\"buildParams\":{\"strict\":true,\"breaks\":false,\"allowHTML\":true,\"singlePage\":true,\"apply-presets\":true,\"langs\":[\"ru\",\"en\"]},\"project-name\":\"dev-metrika\",\"is-new-external\":true,\"no-index\":false,\"langs\":[\"ru\",\"en\"],\"metrika\":19792447,\"favicon-src\":\"https://yastatic.net/s3/metrika/ui-projects/public/_/favicon.ico\"},\"viewerInterface\":{},\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eImporting data into Yandex Metrica offers you more comprehensive insights into users or traffic sources from your tag. This can help you associate user actions on the site with their activity off the site, build end-to-end analytics, or assess your expenses across different traffic channels, significantly expanding your data analysis capabilities.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch2 id=\\\"offline-conv\\\"\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"en/data-import/#offline-conv\\\" class=\\\"yfm-anchor yfm-clipboard-anchor\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"\\u003e\\u003c/a\\u003eImporting offline conversions\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eImporting offline conversions allows you to transmit data about user actions outside the site, such as paying for services after filling out a site form. This data can be attributed to site sessions, helping you build end-to-end analytics and optimize your advertising efforts.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"en/management/offline-conv\\\"\\u003eLearn more about uploading offline conversions\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch2 id=\\\"chats\\\"\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"en/data-import/#chats\\\" class=\\\"yfm-anchor yfm-clipboard-anchor\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"\\u003e\\u003c/a\\u003eImporting chats\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eImporting chats helps you track information about messenger chats with customers and attribute them to site sessions. You can evaluate both the number and quality of chats, track goals, and build reports based on the gathered data.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"en/management/chats-transfer-data\\\"\\u003eLearn more about uploading chats\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch2 id=\\\"crm\\\"\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"en/data-import/#crm\\\" class=\\\"yfm-anchor yfm-clipboard-anchor\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"\\u003e\\u003c/a\\u003eImporting data from a CRM\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eImporting data from your CRM allows you to upload information about customers and their orders to Yandex Metrica. This allows for a more detailed analysis based on order data and transaction statuses, and enables you to create segments for targeting.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"en/data-import/contacts\\\"\\u003eLearn more about uploading data from a CRM\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch2 id=\\\"call\\\"\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"en/data-import/#call\\\" class=\\\"yfm-anchor yfm-clipboard-anchor\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"\\u003e\\u003c/a\\u003eImporting calls\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eImporting calls helps you track information about customer phone calls and attribute them to site sessions. You can evaluate both the number and quality of calls, track goals, and build reports based on the gathered data.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"en/management/calls\\\"\\u003eLearn more about uploading calls\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch2 id=\\\"expense\\\"\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"en/data-import/#expense\\\" class=\\\"yfm-anchor yfm-clipboard-anchor\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"\\u003e\\u003c/a\\u003eImporting expenses\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eImporting data about expenses allows you to compare advertising costs with campaign results, helping you optimize your budget and measure your return on investment (ROI).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"en/management/openapi/expense/uploadMultipart\\\"\\u003eLearn more about uploading expenses\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch2 id=\\\"user-params\\\"\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"en/data-import/#user-params\\\" class=\\\"yfm-anchor yfm-clipboard-anchor\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"\\u003e\\u003c/a\\u003eImporting user parameters\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eYou can import demographic, behavioral, and other user data for more accurate audience segmentation and user behavior analysis.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"en/management/openapi/user_params/upload\\\"\\u003eLearn more about importing user parameters\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\",\"title\":\"Introduction\",\"headings\":[{\"title\":\"Importing offline conversions\",\"href\":\"en/data-import/#offline-conv\",\"level\":2},{\"title\":\"Importing chats\",\"href\":\"en/data-import/#chats\",\"level\":2},{\"title\":\"Importing data from a CRM\",\"href\":\